{"name":"Blue Streak","hex":"#2266bb","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue streak","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A great deal of fast talking, cursing, lying, or similar.","A trace left by something that is too quick to see."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_streak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Streak (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Streak is an album by American blues guitarist Luther Allison, released in 1995 by Alligator Records. Guitar World magazine named it one of the top guitar records of 1995. The album enabled Allison to win five W. C. Handy Awards in 1996, including Contemporary Blues Album for Blue Streak and Blues Song for \"Cherry Red Wine\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/79/Blue_Streak_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Streak_(album)","wikidata":"Q4929926"},{"title":"Blue Streak (missile)","blocks":[{"text":"The de Havilland Propellers Blue Streak was a British Intermediate-range ballistic missile (IRBM), and later the first stage of the Europa satellite launch vehicle. Blue Streak was cancelled without entering full production."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Blue_Streak.JPG/330px-Blue_Streak.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Streak_(missile)","wikidata":"Q885899"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg/500px-Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg/1280px-Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg/500px-Blue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The placard reads: \"Remains of the first Blue Streak rocket launched from Woomera 5 June 1964. Discovered 50 km SE of Giles in 1980.\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Streak_rocket_remains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg/500px-Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg/1280px-Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg/500px-Bluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue Streak F4 rocket remains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABluestreak_in_WoomeraPark.jpg"},{"srcset":"ht